{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Second remediation pass (2026-08-21). The previous pass corrected the plan's false claim that the completion gate bypasses the ref resolver. Verifying the REST of the plan against the tree disproved BOTH consolidations it proposed, so the story has been re-scoped. Evidence:\n\n1. \"The four gates each answer enabled?/what ref?/fail mode? separately.\" FALSE on all three axes.\n   - Ref/source: uniform already. Every gate resolves through `gate_source.resolve_gate_handle` (`src/rebar/llm/gate_source.py:76`); completion reaches it at `src/rebar/llm/completion.py:149` via `close_autoresume.py:234`.\n   - Enabled: three of the four gates already call ONE function, `gates.gate_enabled` (`src/rebar/_commands/gates.py:91`) — from `gates.py:196` (plan-review close), `gates.py:282` (plan-review claim) and `close_precheck.py:489` (completion close).\n   - Fail mode on an unreadable config: uniform already, and the plan described it wrongly. The plan said `gate_enabled` is \"tri-state\" and \"warns to stderr\". It is neither: `GateState` (`gates.py:49`) is two-state — the `UNREADABLE` member was REMOVED by operator ruling 39f8-ae7c — and an unreadable config RAISES a chained `ConfigError` (`gates.py:115-120`). The plan's cited line ranges for that behaviour (`gates.py:127-138`, `gates.py:286,292-297`) point at unrelated code: 127-138 is the `gate_ran` predicate docstring and 292 is `description_cap_warning`. The `unavailable` dict the plan attributed to config unreadability is at `gates.py:264-271` and comes from an ATTESTATION-verify failure, not from the config read; a DISABLED close gate returns a `\"disabled\"` payload (`gates.py:207-212`).\n   What actually differs between the three callers is only what each does with a DISABLED answer — `False`, a `\"disabled\"` payload, `\"gate_off\"`. That is caller policy, not a duplicated computation, and the fourth reader (`code_review_enabled`, `gate_dispatch.py:277`) must keep its divergent broad-`except`/silent-`False` posture, as the plan itself required. A `GatePolicy` resolver would therefore be pure indirection over an already-shared implementation: no copy removed, no behaviour changed. Dropped.\n\n2. \"Replace the twelve in-scope guards with a composite action.\" MECHANICALLY IMPOSSIBLE, not merely awkward. Any shared owner — composite action, shell helper, Python module — lives in the repo, so it is absent on exactly the pre-gate trees the guard exists to serve. On such a tree the guard would skip gates whose scripts DO exist, or the job would fail resolving a missing local `uses:`. This is pinned locally, not inferred: `tests/unit/test_ci_workflow_parity.py::test_mutation_selector_skips_pre_gate_trees_without_weakening_current_trees` executes the step's `run:` body under bash in an EMPTY directory and asserts exit 0 plus the message on stdout (confirmed passing in this worktree); a body that shells out to a repo file cannot satisfy it. `gerrit-verify.yaml:206` already carries the same exposure for `uses: ./.github/actions/docs-gates` itself.\n\n3. Guard count re-verified: 24 occurrences of \"change predates the gate\" across `.github/` — `docs-gates/action.yml` x10, `_build-and-test.yml` x8, `_optionality.yml` x3, `gerrit-verify.yaml` x2, `_mutation.yml` x1. The plan's per-site line numbers were checked and are correct (they point at the `if [ -f … ]` line, not the echo).\n\n4. NEW FINDING while auditing: the guard is applied unevenly. `test.yml:174,176` invoke `scripts/check_readme_quickstart.py` and `scripts/probe_rebar.py` with no guard, while `gerrit-verify.yaml:324,333` guard those same two scripts. That is the shape of bug `anxious-resistant-urson` (a gate step with no guard) recurring.\n\nRe-scope: since the construct cannot have a shared implementation, the achievable equivalent of consolidation is ONE OWNER FOR THE INVARIANT — the epic's own durable shape, a test a drifting copy cannot pass. The story now delivers a parity test asserting (a) every gate step in the six affected files carries an `-f` guard for the file it needs or a mandatory-reason `# predates-gate-ok:` marker, and (b) every guarded step body exits 0 with the canonical message when run in an empty directory, generalising the single-site mutation pin to all of them. Workflow edits are comment-only; genuine gaps are filed and linked rather than fixed here, because adding a guard changes which gates run on an old base and is its own reviewable change.\n\nNote for the epic's net-negative-lines criterion: this child adds a test and removes no lines, because the consolidation it was scoped for was proven unavailable. It should be counted with the hardening children, not the consolidation children."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787376739136082001,"uuid":"b15aa0b3-f27f-4bb5-b8c5-a8b9e671ef41"}